{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544317-which-one","id":1544317,"title":"Which one.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-10-19T16:36:47-07:00","id":1544317,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Which one.","content":"For a VTM video game, what do you think works best? One where you can customize your character, including choosing your clan, or one with a pre-defined character?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-19T16:55:40-07:00","id":1544320,"title":"","content":"There's too many styles of video game to pick which is better. Some styles of game work better with a single predefined character, and others work better with customization of various levels. VtM being the setting doesn't really change that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-19T17:05:21-07:00","id":1544323,"title":"","content":"Heavy Arms' comment is pretty spot on but I think I can add a little bit more to it.<br /> <br /> A fully customizable game with appearance and character options is very cool as long as the game allows for that customization to tailor your gaming experience. Making that experience happen would require an incredibly robust development structure. Each clan, discipline, skill, and background would have to be accounted for and meaningful. This would require the game to be in the AAA range of development or, at the very least, a studio on the verge of becoming a big player in the industry.<br /> <br /> The premade character is a much easier lift as far as development goes. <i>Shadows of New York</i> is a perfect example of this. The story isn't your OC's story, it's Julia Sowinski's and the game's narrative and play are stronger for it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6423-higgipedia","name":"higgipedia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6423_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"53"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-19T17:13:50-07:00","id":1544325,"title":"","content":"It really depends on the type of game play you want from the video game. A predetermined character means the storyline is hand crafted and less likely to be generic, while the ability to select everything about your character provides more personal immersion but a shallower experience because every quest has to deal with every possible character build. A good example of this is comparing VtM: Redemption to VtM: Bloodlines. Both are great games, but they do different things.<br /> <br /> I think that since no one can agree on what makes a good storyline in the current year, should it have a political ideology or not, is representation the top priority, etc. It would probably be a better bet from a strictly technical standpoint to make a video game that allows you to customize your character but engage in a non-first-person method. Think something like a VtM Frost Punk city builder, a VtM Total War RTS, VtM Dungeon Keeper, VtM Evil Bank Manager, etc. You have all the narrative choices of a visual novel which should satisfy the role players, but then there is actual game play to engage those who want to play the vampire rather than just passively empathizing with it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-19T17:54:38-07:00","id":1544328,"title":"","content":"Okay, first of all I apologize for being so vague with my question. I didn't really think at the time that VTM, if you really wanted it to be, could fit into pretty much anything. (In fact, talking about it made me wish there was a VTM with at least a decent stealth strategy, but that's just a daydream on my part.) That said, my intention was just to ask about the open world genres, visual novel style or even something more like dishonored or the shadowrun trilogy, if you get the reference.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-19T20:12:09-07:00","id":1544337,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Psicose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544317-which-one?p=1544328#post1544328\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I didn't really think at the time that VTM, if you really wanted it to be, could fit into pretty much anything.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which is one of its greatest strengths. VtM can be high fantasy, low fantasy, Pulp action, Noire, 80s action movie, heist movie, thriller, or any of a few dozen other things. As such every group plays it a little differently, ignoring whole sections of the setting and focusing on others. Each variation of focus basically turns VtM into a completely different type of video game.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Psicose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544317-which-one?p=1544328#post1544328\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, my intention was just to ask about the open world genres, visual novel style or even something more like dishonored or the shadowrun trilogy, if you get the reference.</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is that the character build determines the genre. A combat build wants to be face to face, while a Background focused social character will want to use minions and lawyers from a distance. Imagine playing Deus Ex: Human Revolution but you can handle the entire game as either Adam Jensen or as his boss working behind the scenes.<br /> <br /> The best generic game answer I would say is that the game starts as a standard RPG for the first half. This way you build your character how you want, raise through the ranks and gain power as is standard. Then at the halfway point you overthrow the Prince and take power. Now you have to deal with a shift in game mechanics and deal with both politics and resource management. Broadly speaking what clan you are and how you built your character gives you different rulership options. You then start to shape the city to your desires and defend it from incursions.<br /> <br /> I would find the above fun to play, but if you are a visual novel RP type, then playing a Deus Ex with Fangs for the first half and a city management game for the second half would probably be akin to an &quot;enhanced interrogation&quot; technique for getting someone to talk.<br /> <br /> Come to think of it, I think I just described Fable III if it was good.....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-20T07:29:03-07:00","id":1544346,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544317-which-one?p=1544337#post1544337\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Which is one of its greatest strengths. VtM can be high fantasy, low fantasy, Pulp action, Noire, 80s action movie, heist movie, thriller, or any of a few dozen other things. As such every group plays it a little differently, ignoring whole sections of the setting and focusing on others. Each variation of focus basically turns VtM into a completely different type of video game.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was talking about genre in the sense of style, with the urban fantasy theme you can do practically anything, even if some would look strange. Have you ever imagined a VTM fighting game?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544317-which-one?p=1544337#post1544337\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The best generic game answer I would say is that the game starts as a standard RPG for the first half. This way you build your character how you want, raise through the ranks and gain power as is standard. Then at the halfway point you overthrow the Prince and take power. Now you have to deal with a shift in game mechanics and deal with both politics and resource management. Broadly speaking what clan you are and how you built your character gives you different rulership options. You then start to shape the city to your desires and defend it from incursions.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would find it interesting, but it would require courage to take that risk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-20T08:46:28-07:00","id":1544347,"title":"","content":"It'd probably be best that Parawolf doesn't over-leverage itself with a large video game project like bloodlines 2 for a long time. If anything, sticking to the small, less costly visual novel games it's been doing on the side would be better.<br /> <br /> WoD in general(whether paradox keeps it or sells it to recoup costs) needs to go in like it's an indie developer trying to drum up a loyal core audience rather than a longstanding IP trying to go mainstream. Paradox has already burned that bridge and whoever owns it in the future shouldn't pretend they can still cross it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-20T11:07:05-07:00","id":1544348,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Psicose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544317-which-one?p=1544346#post1544346\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Have you ever imagined a VTM fighting game?</div> </div> </div> </div>I hadn't considered that before, but off the top of my head it would be a nightmare to get right.<br /> <br /> The easiest way to do it would be to take an existing fighting game and just use VtM skins on the characters and swap out the fighting arena for something WoD themed. But that doesn't make it a VtM fighting game, it is just a skin pack for something else.<br /> <br /> If you were building a VtM fighting game from the ground up, the Disciplines would be very difficult to balance. Obfuscate comes to mind, since it basically means your opponent can't see what you are doing so they have no way to react to it. Though I will admit that the concept of two Obfuscate vampires swinging blindly at each other for an entire match is somewhat amusing.<br /> <br /> But even before dealing with the disciplines powers themselves a fighting game would have the issue of input differences between clans. Using a Brujah as an example, we assume that at the start of the fight they automatically activate both their Potence and Celerity for the scene, which means their button combos really only are for doing things with Presence. That would make a Brujah fighter very simple to use and very powerful, on the other hand a Tremere fighter wouldn't gain anything from Auspex, Dominate is either a 3 second stun or an Auto-win, and then Thaumaturgy is a total mess trying to decide paths and rituals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-20T14:19:43-07:00","id":1544356,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544317-which-one?p=1544348#post1544348\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I hadn't considered that before, but off the top of my head it would be a nightmare to get right.<br /> <br /> The easiest way to do it would be to take an existing fighting game and just use VtM skins on the characters and swap out the fighting arena for something WoD themed. But that doesn't make it a VtM fighting game, it is just a skin pack for something else.<br /> <br /> If you were building a VtM fighting game from the ground up, the Disciplines would be very difficult to balance. Obfuscate comes to mind, since it basically means your opponent can't see what you are doing so they have no way to react to it. Though I will admit that the concept of two Obfuscate vampires swinging blindly at each other for an entire match is somewhat amusing.<br /> <br /> But even before dealing with the disciplines powers themselves a fighting game would have the issue of input differences between clans. Using a Brujah as an example, we assume that at the start of the fight they automatically activate both their Potence and Celerity for the scene, which means their button combos really only are for doing things with Presence. That would make a Brujah fighter very simple to use and very powerful, on the other hand a Tremere fighter wouldn't gain anything from Auspex, Dominate is either a 3 second stun or an Auto-win, and then Thaumaturgy is a total mess trying to decide paths and rituals.</div> </div> </div> </div>It seems difficult because you are restricting yourself to only disciplines in your concept. Taking your Brujah for example, you can take Theo Bell as a concept and make a character fight using a heavy weapon that he carries using Potence and a double-barreled shotgun for support for certain attacks, as a bonus you add some special moves that he does using Celerity. Now the Tremere is even easier to take something from, you can go simple and make a character that fights based on Lure of Flames or Neptune's Might and even extend it to multiple elements, or you can make a character that fights using Movement of Mind on a flying dagger (or several) and has some blows pulling or pushing opponents, you can even do a finisher using Acid Touch from the Hands of Destruction path. It may not work the same way it would in a tablepot game, but you know, poetic liberty.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-20T23:44:01-07:00","id":1544377,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Psicose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544317-which-one?p=1544356#post1544356\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems difficult because you are restricting yourself to only disciplines in your concept.</div> </div> </div> </div>I used the Disciplines as an example because discussing too many different aspects at the same time would get confusing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Psicose</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544317-which-one?p=1544356#post1544356\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Taking your Brujah for example, you can take Theo Bell as a concept and make a character fight using a heavy weapon that he carries using Potence and a double-barreled shotgun for support for certain attacks, as a bonus you add some special moves that he does using Celerity. Now the Tremere is even easier to take something from, you can go simple and make a character that fights based on Lure of Flames or Neptune's Might and even extend it to multiple elements, or you can make a character that fights using Movement of Mind on a flying dagger (or several) and has some blows pulling or pushing opponents, you can even do a finisher using Acid Touch from the Hands of Destruction path. It may not work the same way it would in a tablepot game, but you know, poetic liberty.</div> </div> </div> </div>To me most of that reduces the VtM down to a skin rather than anything that makes the fighting game uniquely VtM, kind of like how all pinball games are the same game play but with different artwork. Admittedly the last two fighting games I played were Street Fighter II and the first Mortal Kombat game when they came out back in the early 90s.<br /> <br /> Celerity alone would almost require a game play system like Y.O.M.I. Hustle, but if you consider the social clans that fight using Backgrounds, then something along the lines of a deck builder mechanic would be more appropriate or perhaps a minion battler &quot;rogue like&quot; such as Darkest Dungeon which has your ghouls and mortal minions fighting it out. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-21T07:12:17-07:00","id":1544394,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544317-which-one?p=1544377#post1544377\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I used the Disciplines as an example because discussing too many different aspects at the same time would get confusing.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> To me most of that reduces the VtM down to a skin rather than anything that makes the fighting game uniquely VtM, kind of like how all pinball games are the same game play but with different artwork. Admittedly the last two fighting games I played were Street Fighter II and the first Mortal Kombat game when they came out back in the early 90s.</div> </div> </div> </div>Worse, I agree, but fighting games are kind of like that most of the time, there's no way to innovate much.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1544317-which-one?p=1544377#post1544377\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Celerity alone would almost require a game play system like Y.O.M.I. Hustle, but if you consider the social clans that fight using Backgrounds, then something along the lines of a deck builder mechanic would be more appropriate or perhaps a minion battler &quot;rogue like&quot; such as Darkest Dungeon which has your ghouls and mortal minions fighting it out.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this falls into the same problem as fighting games, besides the fact that it would be really cool to play with vampires, I've already mentioned this in another post, but if I were to make a VTM game along these lines, I'd like something more like a strategic RPG, where you control a group of kindred dominating/defending territories, you could even include a mechanic where you employ Ghouls or minions to give you support or another to apply disturbances.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-21T10:22:53-07:00","id":1544405,"title":"","content":"It seems like there's a distinction to be made between &quot;if you could only pick one, what kind of game would you make for VtM?&quot; and &quot;how can the VtM brand/IP add to existing game genres with its own entry into them?&quot;<br /> <br /> It's also worth ignoring whether or not a VtM video game would be a big hit. Since fighting games is still a subject, the big 3 of fighting games are (still) Mortal Kombat, Smash Bros, and Tekken. While it's not as big of a step down to the next tier of fighting games as it is from D&amp;D to other TTRPGs, it's still a very noticeable drop off. Far more people could probably name characters from Street Fighter than Tekken, but Street Fighter hasn't really been a big game since SF II (and the plethora of editions of the same game). That doesn't mean SF 3-6 have been flops by any means, it just means it's a more niche game along side DoA and Marvel vs. Capcom (both also still on going successful franchises in their own right despite being second tier ones).<br /> <br /> Most video game genres are dominated by a small number of big franchises. Everything else is a short lived (no matter how well loved) diversion for gamers that want a break from the same-old, or a game catering to a specific niche and settling in their for as long as possible.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-10-24T07:42:59-07:00","id":1544648,"title":"","content":"As was said, the genre really defines whether or not the protagonist should be pre-defined (although there's still room for argument).<br /> <br /> I would generally assume that, as a TTRPG, a video game adaptation would be a CRPG, and if it's an RPG - be that the visual novel type or Bloodlines - I need some kind of customization.If I'm taking full control of the character and chosing their dialogue options and opinions then I get to make them mine in almost all ways, as far as I'm concerned. Some pre-established backstory elements to tie the pc to the plot is perfectly fine with me, as is limiting the clan selection so you can make that matter more, but I find it easier to step into the role I'm playing if I can fudge around with the details to make the pc my own and if I can't then expect me to pass the game over.<br /> <br /> An action or action-adventure game, like a grand theft auto clone, or a survival horror or something with a mostly linear story-line (maybe with multiple endings depending on how you played) where the player has no significant input probably works better with an established protagonist or protagonists, although a little bit of clothes shopping could go in there.<br /> <br /> I do remember a discussion about a VtM Harvest Moon/Stardew valley farming sim parody. 'Caine was a farmer, right? Vampires are basically farmers.' Of course considering the content it'd be a really dark one. And suddenly the human trafficking and crap you do with blood dolls is so much darker when the entire focus of VtM is narrowed down to humans as cattle and turning a profit off of that. (I still prefer customization in my farming sims, but they're not required as in rpgs.)<br /> <br /> A city builder could work, in theory. You are the prince and in your hands you (somehow) hold the strings of the local government and any other required offices. Handle the human concerns that are standard for these games <i>and</i> the vampire concerns in your city planning. Natural disasters could include things along the lines of: 'oopsie daisy looks like that stretch of unbuilt forest you just bought up and turned into the tourism district was a Caern and The Lupines Are Unhappy.' Add Frostpunk style resources and approval meter: if it runs out then you're getting ousted or publicly executed. Maybe sprinkle some politics on top, so you have politicians to manage too. That one would likely have a faceless protagonist, so no customization needed.<br /> <br /> And, of course, there's the option where Paradox could've played to their former(?) strengths and did a Crusader Kings II style political strategy game, but better tailored to vampire politics than modding the hell out of CK (not that the mods are bad). Considering you're playing a theoretically immortal being rather than a human dynasty, it might warrant a character creator, at least for the initial character (after which groom childer to have the traits and appearance you want in case of Final Death when they become your avatar).<br /> <br /> I don't really see much point in a fighting game or beat 'em up for this particular gameline. You could do one, but I agree that slapping some VtM aesthetics and names on a game in the genre doesn't really make it a VtM game, as such. It could be any game, it just has a coat of paint on it.<br /> <br /> A life simulator could be funny (unlife simulator?). Make your town, sink a whole evening into designing your vampire and then another into decorating your haven. Forget what day it is. Manage your needs (including hunger and a masquerade rating) and relationships, half of which are untrustworthy and the other half maintained through blood bonds and abuse of disciplines. Accidentally setting the house ablaze with a low cooking skill is replaced with hunters doing it for you (also the fact that your characters freak out and refuse to let you stop them doing that is slightly justified). No need to install mods for violence and murder, they're already part of core gameplay. Random story progression apparently just had your ally diablerised by your sire and some random npc embraced the mortal you wanted, whoops. Obviously if for some reason a Sims clone was made then it'd be all about customization.<br /> (I recommend the alternative universe that decides to take that on use the Sims 2 as inspiration, possibly including some of the non-pc games. I remember the PSP and DS versions being... interesting.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><sup><i>Prone to hyperbole</i></sup>","edited":{"userid":26836,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","user":"Rhywbeth","date":". 10-24-2024, 11:06 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26836-rhywbeth","name":"Rhywbeth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26836_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"377"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}